  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nTemperatures at 1 PM were running 10 to 18 degrees warmer than \nyesterday in the Inland Empire, 5 to 7 degrees warmer than yesterday \nfor other inland locations, and were within a few degrees of \nyesterday for the coast. Most locations will see an increase in \ntemperatures each day through Saturday, with high temperatures 5 to \n7 degrees above average for inland locations and 7 to 12 degrees \nabove average for the coast. Overnight lows will increase to the mid-\n60s to 70 west of the mountains and in the upper 70s to low 80s in \nthe low desert. Moderate HeatRisk is expected along the coast Friday \nand Saturday and in the low deserts for the second half of the week. \nModerate HeatRisk has the most impacts on those sensitive to heat, \nespecially those without cooling/hydration. \n\nFor Thursday, a closed low lingering to our south will start to \nmove east. This low will draw moisture into Southern California \nfrom the southeast increasing chances for precipitation, mainly in\nthe mountains. Latest high resolutions guidance indicates \nrainfall rates will mostly be 0.20-0.30 inches per hour, where \nthunderstorms develop rates could be closer to 0.50 inches per \nhour. There remains a 5-10% chance of thunderstorms over the \nmountains with the higher chances over the San Diego and Riverside\nCounty mountains. \n\nFor Sunday, an incoming trough of low pressure is expected to move \ninland across northern California. The low will dig south Monday and \nbe to our east Tuesday or early Wednesday. There is good agreement \nthat temperatures will cool 3 to 8 degrees between Saturday and \nSunday and another 2 to 4 degrees for Monday. Additionally cloud \ncoverage will increase. The trough looks like it will pull in \nmoisture from our southwest, but there remains uncertainty in how \nmuch moisture will actually get pulled into Southern California. \nEnsemble members from both the GFS and ECMWF are showing increases\nin precipitable water and some potential for precipitation. \n\nFor next Tuesday and Wednesday, the forecast solutions diverge with \n60% of solutions showing ridging and 40% maintaining weak troughing. \nCurrent forecast follows the NBM and shows a few degrees of warming \neach day Tuesday and Wednesday.",
